// =============================================================================
// stocks-course — "Stocks: What You Actually Own When You Buy a Share"
// =============================================================================
// Darrell 2026-09-19, opening a department this school did not have: "Stock
// Market courses to explore and explain the world of stock and bonds and
// countries that trade and how investment works world wide."
//
// This is course one of four. It answers the first half of the first noun —
// STOCK — and it is deliberately the one that replaces a picture, the way the
// Banking course opens by replacing the drawer. The picture here is that a
// share is a number on a screen that goes up or down. It is not. It is a
// piece of a company, and every confusion downstream grows out of forgetting
// that.
//
// THE ARC, one argument in eight moves:
//   1. A share is a piece of a company      (Matthew 25:14, 15)
//   2. Where shares come from, and why the company gets nothing from your buy (Luke 14:28, 29, 30)
//   3. Between "buy" and "owned"            (Deuteronomy 25:13, 14, 15)
//   4. Price is not value                   (Proverbs 20:14)
//   5. How a share actually pays you        (Matthew 25:16, 17, 27)
//   6. The index is a weighting CHOICE      (Proverbs 11:1)
//   7. Who is on the other side             (Proverbs 11:14)
//   8. The steward's question               (Matthew 25:19, 20, 21, 24, 25; Luke 16:10, 11)
//
// WHY LESSON 2 IS SO EARLY. "When I buy a share, the company gets my money" is
// the most widely held false belief about this subject, and it is held by
// people who have owned shares for years. It is false for almost every trade
// anybody reading this will ever make: the money goes to the person who sold,
// not to the company. A reader who does not have this cannot reason about what
// a share price actually is, what a company gains from a rising one, or why
// anybody cares. So it is taught second, before anything is built on top.
//
// WHY LESSON 6 IS NOT A FOOTNOTE. "The market was up today" is a sentence about
// an average, and averages are built by a WEIGHTING CHOICE. The S&P 500 weights
// by company size; the Dow weights by share price, which means a company can
// move the Dow more than a company ten times its size for no reason but the
// number its shares happen to be priced at. That is a just-weight question in
// the plainest sense (Proverbs 11:1), and it is left untaught almost
// everywhere.
//
// THE LINES THIS COURSE HOLDS (DR-0098 / DR-0100).
//   - Matthew 25 is NOT a stock-market endorsement and the course says so in
//     lesson 1 and again in lesson 8. It is a parable about stewardship of an
//     owner's goods; the servant trades, and the lord names the exchangers, and
//     both facts are IN the text. Building a doctrine of equity investing on
//     top of it is exactly the over-reach DR-0098 forbids. What the passage
//     gives is the FRAME: it is not yours, you will be reckoned with, and
//     burying it is the condemned option.
//   - Documented harm is stated plainly and not hedged (DR-0100). Retail
//     investors trading frequently underperform; that is not a rumour and the
//     course says it without inflating it into "the market is rigged".
//   - No live figure is quoted. Prices, index levels and market sizes move
//     daily; a number printed in a lesson is wrong by the time it is read, and
//     a reader who catches one wrong number rightly distrusts the rest. Every
//     quantitative claim here is either a RULE (T+1 settlement, SEC Rule
//     15c6-1, effective 28 May 2024) or an arithmetic example labelled as one.
//
// VERIFICATION (DR-0076 / SCRIPTURE-REFERENCE-STANDARD). Every quoted span is
// fetched VERBATIM from the repository's own KJV. Our prose says Yahweh;
// quotations keep "God" and "the LORD" exactly as the KJV has them (DR-0210).
//
// TEACHING, NOT FINANCIAL ADVICE -- carried on the meta.
// =============================================================================

