    bigIdea: 'Here is the single most widely held false belief about this subject, held confidently by people who have owned shares for thirty years: that buying a share sends money to the company. It does not. Almost every trade any ordinary person will ever make happens on the SECONDARY market — you buy from another owner, and your money goes to that owner. The company is not a party to it and receives nothing. A company raises money from its shares exactly twice in the ordinary case: when it first sells them, and when it issues more. Everything after that is owners trading among themselves. Once you have this, several confusing things become simple at once — including why a company cares about its share price at all, which is a genuinely good question with a real answer.',
