    lesson: 'This session walks the gap between tapping a button and owning something, because every step in that gap is a measuring system and none of it is shown to the person transacting. START WITH THE TWO LISTS. An order arrives at a trading venue where resting offers to buy and resting offers to sell face each other, each ranked by price. The highest standing offer to buy is the BID. The lowest standing offer to sell is the ASK. There is always a gap between them, because nobody sells for less than they can get. THAT GAP IS THE SPREAD, and it is the first thing a reader should be able to see on a screen. It is never billed as a charge and it is never zero, and it is income to whoever stands ready to take the other side of a trade — the function commonly called market making, which is genuinely a service and genuinely paid for out of your pocket. Both halves of that sentence are true and a course that gives only one is teaching a side. THE SPREAD IS THE PRICE OF LIQUIDITY. On a heavily traded company it may be a penny on a fifty-pound share, a fifth of one percent of nothing. On something rarely traded it can be wide enough that buying and selling in the same minute loses a real sum, with no price movement at all and nobody doing anything wrong. There simply are not many people wanting to trade it, so the person willing to stand in the middle wants paying for standing there. That is the honest cost of owning anything few people want, and it is not disclosed anywhere a casual reader would look — which is why this lesson has the reader measure it themselves rather than take it on report. A MATCH IS NOT OWNERSHIP. After the match comes CLEARING, the confirmation and netting of what each side now owes the other, and then SETTLEMENT, the actual movement of the security into one account and the cash into the other. In the United States the standard settlement cycle is now ONE BUSINESS DAY, having previously been two: the Securities and Exchange Commission shortened it under amendments to Rule 15c6-1, effective 28 May 2024. That is a dated rule, not a rounded recollection, and it is stated with its date because a course that quotes a number it cannot source is training its readers to accept numbers they cannot source. WHY THE WINDOW LENGTH MATTERS is the part worth understanding: for as long as it is open, both sides carry the risk that the other fails to perform, so money has to be set aside against that possibility — money held by somebody in the middle, not usable for anything else, purely so that a failure can be covered. The trade calls money set aside like that COLLATERAL. Halving the window reduces what must be held. An enormous amount of unglamorous work went into a change that is invisible from the outside, which is what plumbing is. AND NOW THE WORD, which speaks to this more directly than to most modern subjects, because a market IS a bag of weights. "Thou shalt not have in thy bag divers weights, a great and a small." (Deuteronomy 25:13) The same again for the house: "Thou shalt not have in thine house divers measures, a great and a small." (Deuteronomy 25:14) And then, rather than stopping at a prohibition, the positive command with a promise fastened to it: "But thou shalt have a perfect and just weight, a perfect and just measure shalt thou have: that thy days may be lengthened in the land which the LORD thy God giveth thee." (Deuteronomy 25:15) A bag with two weights is exactly a market that shows one price to the person who understands the machinery and another to the person who does not. The remedy Scripture gives is not to abolish the bag. It is to make the weight just — and, for the person on the other side of the counter, to know what a weight is. That is what this lesson has done.',
