        body: 'A man taught the parable of the talents at a men’s breakfast and asked, before reading it, what the third servant had done wrong. Nine of eleven said he lost the money. One said he wasted it. One was not sure. Then they read it: he was afraid, and he went and hid it in the earth, and he handed it back whole. Not a penny gone. Perfectly preserved. There was a long pause and one of the men said, well — that is what I have done with the last eleven years. He was not talking about money. He said he had a trade he had never taught anybody, and a thing he had been asked to do at the church three times and had said no to three times, and he had told himself each time that at least he was not making a mess of it. He said the word that went through him was "preserved". He had preserved it. He read the verse again at home that night and told his wife that he had always read the parable as being about losing, and it was not about losing at all.',
