    lesson: 'A profitable company must decide what to do with the money, and the question is almost never put to shareholders plainly. There are three doors, and this session names all three because the third is where a great deal of the modern answer lies and most people have never had it explained to them. THE FIRST DOOR IS RETENTION. The earnings stay inside the business and are spent — on plant, premises, hiring, research, acquisition. Where the business can earn a strong return on that capital this is decisively the best door, because a pound retained and well deployed compounds, and an owner who never saw the money is nonetheless richer for it. Where the business CANNOT earn a strong return — where it reinvests into a shrinking trade because the directors would rather run a larger company than a better one — retention is quiet destruction, and it is genuinely hard to see from outside. Both cases look identical on a statement. THE SECOND DOOR IS THE DIVIDEND. Cash is paid out to the owners, ordinarily on a regular cycle. It is visible, it is spendable, and for a great many people it is the entire reason to own shares. It carries a discipline retention does not: a board that has committed to a dividend must find the cash every period, in public, and cannot quietly fund a bad decision out of earnings nobody is examining. THE THIRD DOOR IS THE BUYBACK, and it is the one to slow down on. The company buys its own shares in the market and cancels them. NOTHING ARRIVES IN ANYBODY’S ACCOUNT. But the number of shares in existence falls, so every remaining holder now owns a larger fraction of the same business. That is real value reaching the owner by a route that leaves no trace anywhere a person would ordinarily look, and the single way to see it is to check whether the share count has fallen over the years. It has become a principal channel, and a person watching only for dividends will conclude that a company returns nothing to its owners while it is in fact returning a great deal. TWO HONEST QUALIFICATIONS BELONG IN THE SAME BREATH, and this course gives both rather than the convenient one. A buyback at a low price is excellent for the remaining owners; a buyback at an inflated price destroys their value exactly as any other overpayment does, because the company is a buyer like any other and the proverb from the last lesson applies to it too. And because senior remuneration is frequently tied to per-share measures, there is a standing temptation to shrink the share count for reasons that serve the board rather than the owners. Say both. A course that gives only the first is a brochure and a course that gives only the second is a grievance. UNDERNEATH ALL THREE DOORS SITS THE PARABLE. "Then he that had received the five talents went and traded with the same, and made them other five talents." (Matthew 25:16) And likewise the second: "And likewise he that had received two, he also gained other two." (Matthew 25:17) The commendation attaches to EMPLOYMENT of what was entrusted. Now the third servant, who is routinely misremembered and whose actual conduct is the sharpest thing in the passage. "But he that had received one went and digged in the earth, and hid his lord’s money." (Matthew 25:18) And in his own words at the reckoning: "And I was afraid, and went and hid thy talent in the earth: lo, there thou hast that is thine." (Matthew 25:25) HE DID NOT LOSE IT. He preserved it perfectly and returned it whole. That was the failure — and the lord’s answer names a floor beneath which even fear was no excuse: "Thou oughtest therefore to have put my money to the exchangers, and then at my coming I should have received mine own with usury." (Matthew 25:27) This course does not read that as an instruction to invest in equities; that would be the over-reach this house refuses. It reads it as the passage plainly gives it: burial was the condemned option, and doing nothing was not the safe choice the servant believed it was.',
