    lesson: 'Every purchase implies a sale. That is arithmetic rather than insight, and yet almost nobody pauses on it. Somebody, at that exact moment, concluded that parting with this holding, at this price, was the better course — and the honest question follows immediately: who were they, and what did they know? SOMETIMES THE ANSWER IS ENTIRELY BENIGN. A seller may be retiring, meeting a tax bill, paying for a wedding, or rebalancing because a rule in a pension scheme obliged them to. None of that carries information about the company and none of it is a signal to anybody about anything. SOMETIMES IT IS NOT. The seller may have studied the business for months, reached the opposite view, and be right. A person who never asks the question has quietly assumed the first case every time. IT IS WORTH KNOWING THE FOUR BROAD SORTS OF COUNTERPARTY. ORDINARY INDIVIDUALS, in the same position as the reader. INSTITUTIONS — pension funds, insurers, asset managers — moving very large sums, frequently for reasons that have no connection to any view about the enterprise. MARKET MAKERS, who are not forecasting anything at all: they stand ready to take either side, earn the spread discussed in lesson three, and aim to end the day holding as little as possible. And INDEX FUNDS, which transact because a rule obliges them, not because any person formed a judgement. That last category has grown very large, and its implication is stranger than most commentary admits: A GREAT DEAL OF TRADING CONTAINS NO OPINION ABOUT ANYTHING. The price moved, and nobody thought anything. AND NOW A FINDING THIS COURSE STATES PLAINLY RATHER THAN HEDGING, because hedging it would be a kindness that costs people money. INVESTORS WHO TRADE FREQUENTLY TEND TO DO WORSE THAN INVESTORS WHO TRADE RARELY. That is established, it is not a moral judgement about anyone’s intelligence, and it should not be softened into "some research suggests". The mechanism is unremarkable and worth saying out loud so nobody has to take it on authority: every transaction pays a spread and frequently a fee; acting on a strong feeling is usually acting on the crowd’s feeling slightly late, because by the time a thing feels obvious it has been obvious to others for a while; and the person who does nothing incurs no cost whatever. Three ordinary mechanisms, compounding quietly. WHAT FOLLOWS FROM THIS IS NOT WITHDRAWAL. This course has already settled what burial is, in lesson five, and it will not now recommend it under another name. What follows is the instruction Scripture actually gives for decisions taken under uncertainty, and it is more modest and more useful than the way it is usually quoted. "Where no counsel is, the people fall: but in the multitude of counsellors there is safety." (Proverbs 11:14) READ THE PROMISE PRECISELY. It does not say the counsellors will be correct. It does not say a committee decides better than a person. It locates the SAFETY in the MULTITUDE — in the requirement that a decision pass through more than one mind before it becomes an act. A person alone at a screen, holding a strong conviction and a button, is exactly the condition the first clause of that verse describes, and the remedy it offers is not more information. It is more counsel. Those are different things, and the difference is the whole of this lesson: the man in the second story told four people afterwards and asked none of them beforehand, and he had believed for years that this was independence.',
